<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · Lament · 1709 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Malbrough s'en va-t-en guerre</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A French mock lament from around 1709 for the Duke of Marlborough. Its tune is the one of For He's a Jolly Good Fellow.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>French; later sung across Europe and the Americas</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>France</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>after the Battle of Malplaquet, 1709; widely popular from 1780</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous; written after 1709)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbrough-sen-va-t-en-guerre">Malbrough s'en va-t-en guerre</a>&quot; (&quot;Marlborough is going off to war&quot;), also known as &quot;Mort et convoi de l'invincible Malbrough&quot; (&quot;The death and burial of the invincible Marlborough&quot;), is a French folk song. It is a burlesque lament on the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough">John Churchill, 1st Duke of Marlborough</a> (1650–1722), written on a false rumour of his death after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-malplaquet">Battle of Malplaquet</a> in 1709, the bloodiest battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. It tells how Marlborough's wife, waiting for him to come back from the war, is brought the news that he is dead, how he was buried, and how a nightingale sang over his grave.</p><p>For years it lived only by word of mouth: it is not among the many songs on the events of the day printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the middle of the 18th century. Pierre Beaumarchais set a despairing love song for Cherubino to its tune in his play The Marriage of Figaro (1778), and in 1780 it became very popular; Carl Stamitz ended a sonata for viola d'amore and viola, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> that year, with it. When Prince Louis-Joseph, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a> and heir to the throne, was born in 1781, his wet nurse, a peasant named Geneviève Poitrine, sang it as she rocked the royal cradle. The name, the simple words and the melody caught the queen's interest, and she sang it often; everyone took it up after her, the king included, and it was sung in the state apartments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>, in the kitchens and in the stables. Nicolas Dalayrac quoted it in the first act of his opera Renaud d'Ast (1787).</p><p>From the court it passed to the tradespeople of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and from town to town and country to country. It became as popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe">Johann Wolfgang von Goethe</a> came to hate Marlborough merely because he met the tune everywhere on his travels in France. Through the Bourbon dynasty's influence on the Spanish nobility it spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where Marlborough became the easier &quot;Mambrú&quot; and children sang it while playing hopscotch (rayuela). Jacinto Valledor ended a tonadilla on &quot;the life and death of General Malbrú&quot; with the tune, and in 1826 the guitarist Fernando Sor wrote his Introduction and Variations on it, Op. 28. From Spain it went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>.</p><p>The craze lasted for years and faded slowly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> is said to have hummed the tune, among other times when crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Memel</a> in June 1812 at the start of his fatal Russian campaign. Beethoven used it to stand for the French in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a>'s Victory. In the United Kingdom the melody took on new words: by the middle of the 19th century it was sung as &quot;For He's a Jolly Good Fellow&quot;, often at gatherings of men, and by 1862 those words were familiar in America too, where the same melody also became the campfire song &quot;The Bear Went Over the Mountain&quot;. The song itself has been translated into several languages, among them an English version published by the American poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-wadsworth-longfellow">Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</a>.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A mock funeral song. Marlborough rides off to war and does not come back; his lady waits for him in vain until a messenger brings word that he is dead and tells of his burial, with a nightingale singing over the grave. The bouncing tune and nonsense refrain turn the lament for a feared enemy general into cheerful mockery.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">French</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Malbrough s’en va-t-en guerre,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Malbrough s’en va-t-en guerre,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ne sait quand reviendra…</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Il reviendra z’-à Pâques,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Il reviendra z’-à Pâques,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ou à la Trinité.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">La Trinité se passe,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">La Trinité se passe,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Malbrough ne revient pas.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Madame à sa tour monte,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Madame à sa tour monte,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Si haut qu’ell’ peut monter.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 88 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Elle aperçoit son page,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Elle aperçoit son page,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tout de noir habillé.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Beau page ! Ah ! Mon beau page,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Beau page ! Ah ! Mon beau page,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Quell’ nouvelle apportez ?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Aux nouvell’s que j’apporte,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Aux nouvell’s que j’apporte,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Vos beaux yeux vont pleurer.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Quittez vos habits roses,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Quittez vos habits roses,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et vos satins brochés.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Monsieur d’Malbrough est mort,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Monsieur d’Malbrough est mort,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Est mort et enterré.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">J’l’ai vu porté en terre,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">J’l’ai vu porté en terre,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Par quatre z-officiers.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’un portait sa cuirasse,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’un portait sa cuirasse,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’autre son bouclier.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’un portait son grand sabre,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’un portait son grand sabre,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’autre ne portait rien.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">À l’entour de sa tombe,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">À l’entour de sa tombe</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Romarin l’on planta.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sur la plus haute branche,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sur la plus haute branche,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le rossignol chanta.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">On vit voler son âme,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">On vit voler son âme,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">À travers les lauriers.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Chacun mit ventre à terre,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Chacun mit ventre à terre,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et puis se releva.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour chanter les victoires,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour chanter les victoires,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Que Malbrough remporta.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">La cérémonie faite,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">La cérémonie faite,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Chacun s’en fut coucher.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Les uns avec leurs femmes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Les uns avec leurs femmes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et les autres tout seuls.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ce n’est pas qu’il en manque,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ce n’est pas qu’il en manque,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Car j’en connais beaucoup.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Des blondes et des brunes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Des blondes et des brunes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et des châtaign’s aussi.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">J’ n’en dis pas davantage,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">J’ n’en dis pas davantage,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Car en voilà z-assez.</span></p></div></details></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Malbrouck has gone a-fighting,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Malbrouck has gone a-fighting</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>But when will he return?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Perchance he'll come at Easter</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Or else at Trinity Term.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>But Trinity Term is over</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And Malbrouck comes not yet.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>My Lady climbs her watch tower</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>As high as she can get.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>She sees her page approaching</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>All clad in sable hue:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;Ah page, brave page, what tidings</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>From my true lord bring you?&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;The news I bring, fair Lady,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Will make your tears run down;</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 42 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;Put off your rose-red dress so fine</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And doff your satin gown.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;Monsieur Malbrouck is dead, alas!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And buried too, for aye;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;I saw four officers who bore</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>His mighty corse away.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;One bore his cuirass, and his friend</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>His shield of iron wrought;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;The third his mighty sabre bore,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And the fourth—he carried nought.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;And at the corners of his tomb</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>They planted rose-marie;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;And from their tops the nightingale</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rings out her carol free.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;We saw, above the laurels,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>His soul fly forth amain;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;And each one fell upon his face</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And then rose up again.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;And so we sang the glories</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>For which great Malbrouck bled;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;And when the whole was ended</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Each one went off to bed.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;I say no more, my Lady,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mironton, mironton, mirontaine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>I say no more, my Lady,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>As nought more can be said.&quot;</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Le Siècle chantant (13 September 1864). Text from <a href="https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/Marlbrough_s%E2%80%99en_va-t-en_guerre" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: tr. William McLennan, Songs of Old Canada (1886). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Songs_of_Old_Canada%2FMalbrouck" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
