<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Marriage of Figaro (Pierre Beaumarchais)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1778</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, for Cherubino's love song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sonata in D major for viola d'amore and viola (Carl Stamitz)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1780</span><span class="sg-chain-h">tune concludes it</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Musical box of the Negress head clock (Paris)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1784</span><span class="sg-chain-h">one of its eight tunes</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Renaud d'Ast (Nicolas Dalayrac)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1787</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">La cantada vida y muerte del general Malbrú (Jacinto Valledor)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">tune concludes the tonadilla</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Wellington's Victory (Ludwig van Beethoven)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted, for the French</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Introduction and Variations on Marlborough s'en va-t-en guerre, Op. 28 (Fernando Sor)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1826</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations for guitar</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">For He's a Jolly Good Fellow</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">mid-19th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, new words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Bear Went Over the Mountain</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">English translation (Henry Wadsworth Longfellow)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Air Malbrough, variations for harpsichord or fortepiano (Pierre-Louis Couperin)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ode on a Negress Head Clock, with Eight Tunes (Rita Dove)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2009</span><span class="sg-chain-h">named in a poem</span></li></ul></section>
